mike reese espn nfl nation reporter  covering the patriots  and uh also an analyst for espn  boston.com kind enough to join us here  mike good morning thanks for joining us  how would you sum up the last couple of  days with the patriots  well uh you know for the players dan uh  totally topsy-turvy i think saturday  they get the word that  cam newton had tested positive for  covid19  and a lot of questions and a lot of  concerns you know i would say it  highlights to me the human side of the  game  you know initially some of the players  that i had communicated with said  we're really going to go forward and  play this game is it worth the risk of  doing so  and i think as we talk right now maybe  there's been a little more acceptance  among some of the players just to move  forward as time has passed a little bit  has cam spoken he hasn't he just posted  something on instagram  uh yesterday afternoon just saying you  know thank you for the love and support  and that he's going to take this time  to get healthy and that's really all  we've heard from cam  why brian hoyer not jared stood him  tonight  terrific question and really after all  off season  we've been talking how jared stood him  at the inside track right  to be tom brady's heir apparent and  let's say two things dan you know the  first thing when cam came in  july 8th that was like a last minute  type thing and  it was sort of clear like it was going  to be cam as long as it didn't go  sideways  but really everyone thought jarrett  would be the number two and  everything changed for him the fourth  day of training camp he  went to a boston hospital for tests on  his leg and the test came back  negative but when he was on the field  for the next practice  his reps were really diminished and it  sort of went from a three  picture quarterback snapshot to just two  and stidham never really developed any  momentum after that and  never really made a charge to sort of  change the team's mind that  he should be the number two guy so i  think the experience of hoyer here  is something they're a little more  comfortable with after that sort of  changed the fourth day of training camp  and also going on the road you'd  probably want to go with veteran  leadership with uh with hoyer but  how are how are the travel arrangements  different for the patriots for this game  as opposed to a normal game  well how about a 22-hour work day that's  basically what they're looking at check  this out  they woke up this morning before the sun  came up here in new england  they go to their facility to get tested  they board the buses and they head to  two different airports  one in boston one in providence you get  some of the players that have been in  close contact are all the players that  have been deemed to be in close contact  with cam  on one plane going to one airport and  then the other  rest of the team on another plane at a  different airport they're going to  arrive at around the same time  in kansas city later this morning  they'll have about three hours  at a hotel in kansas city then head to  arrowhead stadium for the game  hopefully put up for them you know a  competitive performance  and then hop back on the plane and get  back to gillette stadium let's say  three four in the morning eastern time  about 22 hour work day for them not  ideal but something that i  you know at the players i've  communicated with they've sort of  accepted this as the reality  was there any push by the patriots to  get this as a standalone game on tuesday  not that i have been able to discern in  in the people i've spoken with in fact  if anything they like the idea of  playing it tonight  because of the turnaround they have a  home game sunday  against the broncos and the idea of  playing tuesday  and then getting back in the middle of  the night and having that short  condensed time to get ready for their  next game at home i don't think was  my sense dan was that that wasn't viewed  as an ideal scenario for them  i know you've been covering this team  for a long time and they're there  every day but i've never heard bill  belichick talk about a player  the way he has spoken about cam newton  like the whole month  and i wondered if tom brady went a  couple of years without hearing  the niceties that cam got in one month  what's the logic because there's always  some kind of logic that's calculated  with bill belichick so  why the fawning over cam newton  oh i wish i had the answer like a lot of  things with this team  dan right you know i will say  in general and this is probably more  specific to  bill belichick i have personally noticed  a little bit of a softening  just overall this year not just with cam  but you know everything that's gone on  uh with covid19  he just lost his mother you know within  the last month  and i think just in general maybe the  whole virtual nature of our  interactions with him it's not as much  in his face  you know and i think he feels maybe more  comfortable  in these interactions and if i had to  say one thing like that's probably a  bunch of things but  that might be more of a factor than  anything related to the whole  bradycam comparisons and and you know  what damn maybe there's something to  the idea that he's done some research  with the people that have been around  cam in the past and maybe you know the  idea of building cam up is something  he's  learned from those that have coached cam  before is a good way to go  mike great stuff as always enjoy your  work on the mothership and thanks for  joining us we appreciate it  thanks for having me dan that's mike  reese espn nfl nation reporter covering  the patriots
 

